Welcome to the Misa vi tutorial. This file teaches you how to use misa's
built-in editor. It is meant to be read inside the editor -- try things as
you go. Edits here are harmless; just :q! to quit without saving.

This tutorial is based on smartutor by unix-supremacist.
________________________________________________________________________________

                              Moving your cursor

You can move around in normal mode with the hjkl keys.
h is left.
j is down.
k is up.
l is right.

Try moving around this section before continuing.
________________________________________________________________________________

                                  Exiting vi

To save and exit press ZZ, to exit without saving press ZQ.
The command line alternatives are:
:wq    write and quit
:w     write (save)
:q     quit (fails if unsaved changes)
:q!    force quit without saving
________________________________________________________________________________

                             Exiting to Normal Mode

You can return to normal mode at any time by pressing escape.
________________________________________________________________________________

                                Movement Commands

w goes forward to next word; W goes forward to next WORD (whitespace-delimited).
b goes backwards to last word; B goes backwards to last WORD.
e goes to end of next word; E goes to end of next WORD.
$ and [end] goes to end of line
0 and [home] goes to beginning of line
^ or _ to first non-blank character
H goes to top of screen
M goes to middle of screen
L goes to bottom of screen
+ and [enter] to first character of next line
- to first character of previous line
( to beginning of the current sentence
) to beginning of the next sentence
{ to beginning of the current paragraph
} to beginning of the next paragraph
[[ to beginning of current section
]] to beginning of next section
G goes to last line (or 5G goes to line 5)
gg goes to first line (or 5gg goes to line 5)
________________________________________________________________________________

                                Deletion Commands

Select a character you want to delete like this tygpo and press x to delete it.
X deletes the character before the cursor (like backspace).

You can combine the d command with movement commands like so:
delete a misplaced word like this one one with dw.
or delete the garbage on the end of this line with d$ dasfnaesfnasefas

You can delete until the end of the line with just D as well.

asdnaofnasdoansdan
You can delete an entire line with dd.
________________________________________________________________________________

                              Change/Replace Commands

Select a character you want to replace and press r like in this tylo.

adnaofanf
You can replace an entire line with R, replace this one with "vi is great"

You can combine the c command with movement commands like so:
change a typoed word like this oen with cw.

To change the word under the cursor, even from its middle, use ciw, type the
replacement, then press esc. diw deletes that word; daw also includes adjacent
whitespace. For a whitespace-delimited WORD (including punctuation), use iW
or aW instead. These objects can also be used in Visual mode.

The same idea works for brackets and quotes: ci( changes what is inside the
parentheses around the cursor, da" deletes a quoted string with its spaces.
Use i or a with ( ) b [ ] { } B < > " ' or `.

You can use C to change until end of the line.

You can replace a character and keep typing with s, fix this dpo.

You can replace an entire line with S.
________________________________________________________________________________

                                Insertion Commands

If you press the a key you will enter insert mode after the character you had
selected; type like a normal editor then press esc to exit.

If you press i you will be in insert mode before the character you had selected.

If you press o you will be in insert mode on a new line below your cursor.

If you press A you will be in insert mode at the end of a line.

If you press I you will be in insert mode at the beginning of a line.

If you press O you will be in insert mode on a new line above your cursor.

A terminal paste inserts text literally in Normal or Insert mode; it does not
run the pasted characters as commands. In Insert mode it belongs to the current
insert undo unit. In a command prompt, pasted newlines do not submit the command;
press Enter yourself when the command is ready.
________________________________________________________________________________

                                  Count Commands

You can type a number before any command to repeat it. For example 10x deletes
10 characters, or 3dd deletes 3 lines, or 5j moves down 5 lines.

Counts also work with insert commands: 3oESC creates 3 new lines.
________________________________________________________________________________

                             Undo, Redo, and History

Press u to undo the last change. You can type a count first: 4u undoes 4
changes at once.

Press ctrl+r to redo (undo the undo).

Press U to undo all changes made on the current line since you moved to it.

For navigating edit history by count:
:earlier 5    undo 5 changes
:later 3      redo 3 changes
________________________________________________________________________________

                                  Put Command

p pastes after the cursor (or below the current line for line-wise yanks).
P pastes before the cursor (or above the current line for line-wise yanks).
________________________________________________________________________________

                                  Yank Command

y lets you copy text without deleting it.
yw yanks a word.
yy yanks a line.
Works with all motions: y$ yanks to end of line, y{ yanks to paragraph start.
________________________________________________________________________________

                                 Repeat Command

You can repeat the last editing command by pressing . (dot).
This works with insert, delete, change, replace, indent, and more.
________________________________________________________________________________

                                  Join Command

J joins the current line with the next line.

Press J here to
 join these two lines together.
________________________________________________________________________________

                               Screen Movement

^f moves forwards a screen.
^b moves backwards a screen.
^d moves down half a screen.
^u moves up half a screen.
^e scrolls down one line.
^y scrolls up one line.
z[enter] or zt snaps screen to put cursor on top.
z. or zz snaps screen to put cursor centered.
z- or zb snaps screen to put cursor on bottom.
________________________________________________________________________________

                                   Searching

/ searches forward for text. Type /word and press enter.
? searches backward. Type ?word and press enter.

After a search:
n repeats the search in the same direction.
N repeats the search in the opposite direction.

* and # search forward and backward for the word at the cursor. They match
whole words; g* and g# also match the same text inside a longer word.
These searches become the last search, so n and N repeat them. A count before
one of these commands selects a later match.

Patterns use Vim syntax (default "magic"): . matches any character, * repeats,
\+ \= \{n,m} repeat, ^ and $ anchor, \( \) group, \| alternates, [a-z] is a
collection, \d \w \s are classes, \< and \> mark word edges. \v makes most
of these special without a backslash; \V takes everything literal.
An empty pattern (just Enter) reuses the last pattern. Constructs such as
\zs, \@=, \1 and \n are not supported and report an error.

Case-insensitive search:
  /word\c     forces case insensitive for this search
  /word\C     forces case sensitive for this search
  :set ic     makes all searches case insensitive
  :set noic   makes all searches case sensitive
________________________________________________________________________________

                              Bracket Matching

Press % when on a bracket character to jump to its match.
Works with (), [], and {}. If the cursor is not on a bracket, it scans
forward on the current line to find one.

Try it on these: (hello (world)) or {some [nested] braces}
________________________________________________________________________________

                              Find on Line

f followed by a character finds that character forward on the current line.
F finds backward.
t goes up to (but not on) the character forward.
T goes up to the character backward.

; repeats the last f/F/t/T in the same direction.
, repeats it in the opposite direction.

These work with operators: dt) deletes up to the next closing parenthesis.

Try: delete the parenthetical (this part) using dt) then d2l or df)
________________________________________________________________________________

                            Indent and Dedent

>> indents the current line (adds a tab).
<< dedents the current line (removes a tab or up to 8 spaces).

These work as operators with motions:
>} indents from here to the next paragraph.
<5j dedents the next 5 lines.
>5> indents 5 lines (count works with doubled operator too).
________________________________________________________________________________

                                 Visual Mode

v enters character-wise visual mode. Move the cursor to select text.
V enters line-wise visual mode. Selects whole lines.

Once you have a selection, apply an operator:
d or x     delete selection
c or s     change selection (delete and enter insert mode)
y          yank (copy) selection
>          indent selection
<          dedent selection
~          toggle case of selection
u          lowercase selection
U          uppercase selection
J          join selected lines
:          enter command mode with the selection as a range

Press o to swap which end of the selection the cursor is on.
Press esc to cancel the selection.
________________________________________________________________________________

                               Named Registers

By default, yanks and deletes go to the unnamed register. You can use named
registers to store text in specific slots.

"ayw      yank a word into register a
"ap       paste from register a
"Ayw      append a word to register a (capital letter appends)

Registers a-z are available. The numbered registers 1-9 automatically store
your last 9 line-wise deletes (1 is most recent). Register 0 always holds
the last yank. Register - holds small deletes (less than a line).

To see what is in a register, you can paste from it: "1p pastes the last
line you deleted with dd.
________________________________________________________________________________

                                    Macros

Macros let you record and replay a sequence of keystrokes.

qa        start recording into register a
(do your editing)
q         stop recording

@a        play back macro a
@@        repeat the last played macro
5@a       play macro a five times

Since macros are stored in registers, you can edit them:
1. Paste the macro text: "ap
2. Edit the text
3. Yank it back: "ayy

This is one of the most powerful features in vi -- automate any repetitive
editing task.
________________________________________________________________________________

                                    Marks

Marks let you bookmark positions in a file.

ma        set mark a at the current position
'a        jump to the line of mark a (first non-blank character)
`a        jump to the exact position of mark a (line and column)

Marks a-z are available. Set them anywhere and jump back instantly.
________________________________________________________________________________

                                  Jump List

When you make big jumps (G, gg, /, ?, n, N, %), the editor remembers where
you were.

ctrl+o    jump back to previous position
ctrl+i    jump forward to next position

This is useful when you search for something then want to get back.
________________________________________________________________________________

                               File Information

ctrl+g shows information about the current file: filename, whether it has
been modified, total line count, your position as a percentage, and your
current line and column numbers.
________________________________________________________________________________

                              Substitute Command

:s/old/new      replace first occurrence of "old" with "new" on current line
:s/old/new/g    replace all occurrences on current line
:%s/old/new/g   replace all occurrences in entire file
:5,10s/old/new  replace on lines 5 through 10

Flags:
  g    replace all occurrences per line (not just the first)
  i    case insensitive matching
  c    ask before each replacement

With c, answer y to replace, n to skip, a to replace the remaining matches,
q or Escape to stop, or l to replace this match and stop. Ctrl-E and Ctrl-Y
scroll the viewport while the confirmation is open. Accepted replacements
form one undoable change; substitution does not replace the previous dot repeat.
Confirmation does not support replacements that produce newlines or confirmation
inside :global. :s and :g take the same Vim patterns as /; an empty pattern
(:s//new/) reuses the last search pattern.

The delimiter can be any character: :s|old|new|g works too.
________________________________________________________________________________

                              Global Commands

:g/pattern/command    run command on every line matching pattern
:v/pattern/command    run command on every line NOT matching pattern

The default command is d (delete), so:
:g/TODO/d       deletes all lines containing TODO
:v/keep/d       deletes all lines that don't contain "keep"
:g/old/s/old/new/g   substitute on matching lines only

These are extremely powerful for bulk editing.
________________________________________________________________________________

                           Reading and Shell Commands

:r filename     insert contents of filename below the cursor
:r !date        insert output of shell command "date" below the cursor
:r !ls          insert directory listing below the cursor

:!command       run a shell command and show its output

:%!command      filter the entire file through a command
:%!sort         sort all lines
:%!jq .         format JSON through jq
:5,10!sort      sort only lines 5 through 10
!!              open a filter prompt for this line (3!! for three lines)
!motion         open a filter prompt for the lines covered by a motion

Shell commands use sh -c in the editor's starting directory, not the open file's
directory. They inherit the environment from when the editor opened. They run in
the background while the editor keeps drawing; editing is paused until completion.
Press Ctrl-C to cancel (on Unix this kills the shell's process group).

:! and :w ! show multiline output. Use arrows or Page Up/Down to scroll, then
Enter to return to editing. Stderr is shown too; a nonzero exit reports its status.
Try :!ls to list the starting directory. :!rm FILE removes FILE there: only use
this on a disposable practice file, since shell deletions cannot be undone.
Commands must be available on your system; interactive terminal commands are not
supported here. Output is collected in memory until the command finishes.

Reads preserve blank lines. :0r file inserts before the first line; :3r !command
inserts after line 3. Reading at the end preserves a missing final newline; reading
in the middle needs a separator before the following line. Reads and filters use
the buffer's line-ending format. A successful read or filter is one undo step.
If a filter fails or is cancelled, its input stays unchanged even if it emitted
some output. Shell edits preserve registers and do not replace your dot change.
