                            Line Ranges in Commands

Most ex commands accept a range before them:
.              current line
$              last line
%              all lines (same as 1,$)
5              line 5
'a             line of mark a
.+3            three lines below current
$-2            two lines before last

Examples:
:5d            delete line 5
:3,7y          yank lines 3 through 7
:'a,'bd        delete from mark a to mark b
:.,$s/x/y/g    substitute from current line to end of file
________________________________________________________________________________

                          Operator + Motion Summary

vi's power comes from combining operators with motions.

Operators: d (delete), c (change), y (yank), > (indent), < (dedent)

Each operator can be combined with any motion:
dw       delete word              yw       yank word
d$       delete to end of line    y$       yank to end of line
d}       delete to paragraph end  c}       change to paragraph end
dG       delete to end of file    ygg      yank to start of file
df)      delete through next )    ct"      change up to next "
d%       delete to matching bracket
>}       indent to next paragraph

Double the operator to apply to the whole line: dd, cc, yy, >>, <<

Counts work everywhere: 3dw deletes 3 words, d3w also deletes 3 words,
2d3w deletes 6 words.
________________________________________________________________________________

                          Opening and Reloading Files

:e notes.txt   open another file; :ed, :edi, and :edit also work
:e            reload the current file
:e!           discard unsaved changes and reload; :e! notes.txt forces a switch
:e #          open the alternate filename, if there is one

An unsaved buffer blocks :e with E37, even for the same file. Save first, or use
! only when you intend to discard changes. A nonexistent file opens empty as
[New]. Other read errors leave the current buffer and its history intact.
A successful edit starts at line 1, column 1, clears undo/redo, local marks and
jumps, but keeps registers and the previous dot change. Switching to a different
name records the old name as the alternate; there is no buffer cache.

The entire argument is a filename, so spaces are literal: :e my notes.txt.
% and # in an argument expand to the current and alternate names. ~/ and $VAR
or ${VAR} use the environment captured when the editor started. Variable names
must match [A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*. Malformed or unsupported dollar forms and
unset or empty variables are errors and leave the buffer unchanged. Relative
paths use the editor directory. Shell expansion, globs, ~user, filename modifiers,
and shell-style quoting are not supported. :edit does not accept a line range,
+cmd or ++opt, and cannot be nested inside :global.
________________________________________________________________________________

                              File Safety

A missing file opens as a new buffer. If the initial file cannot be read, the error
is shown and saving requires :w!; check the path before forcing a write. A failed
:edit instead keeps the current buffer unchanged. If the file changed outside
the editor, a normal save is refused. Review the change before using :w!, :wq!,
or :x! to overwrite it. A failed save keeps your edits marked as unsaved.

:w other writes a copy without changing the current filename or clearing its
unsaved state. In an unnamed buffer, a full :w name adopts that name. Use
:saveas name to write and adopt a different name, or :file name to rename the
buffer without writing. Relative paths are resolved from the editor directory.

:2,4w excerpt writes those lines to a file. In Visual mode, press : and use
w excerpt to write the selected lines. A range write never marks the buffer
saved, even if it includes every line. A partial write to the current file
requires !. Use :w >> file to append; creating a missing append target requires
:w! >> file. :w !command sends the lines to the asynchronous shell runner.
A clean buffer stays clean even after a partial write or append changes its
current file. Use a full :w if you want the file to match the buffer again.

Line endings and the presence or absence of a final newline are preserved.
Ordinary files use a temporary file and rename; links and files owned by
another user are written in place, so those saves are not atomic.
